+\subsection{Model Selection Under a Fixed VRAM Budget}
+\label{sec:modelselect}
+
+Two 2026 release candidates were evaluated with the same 90-sentence FLORES-200
+protocol used throughout this paper, under the constraint of an 8\,GB VRAM budget.
+
+\textbf{NiuTrans LMT-60-1.7B} (Qwen3-based, bf16, chat-template prompt,
+num\_beams=5) scored 63.8 BLEU / 78.3 chrF at 86\,chars/s, peaking at 6{,}635\,MiB
+VRAM. It is rejected in favor of the already-deployed MiLMMT-46-1B, which scores
+higher (\MILMMTFLORESBLEU{} vs.\ 63.8 BLEU), uses under half the VRAM (3.4\,GB vs.\
+6.6\,GB), and translates faster (\MILMMTCHS{} vs.\ 86\,chars/s). A methodological
+caution accompanies this result: an initial 5-sentence smoke test of LMT-60 scored
+73.2 BLEU, 9.4 points higher than the 90-sentence measurement (63.8). Small-sample
+BLEU is noisy enough to reverse a model-selection decision; the 90-sentence figure,
+not the smoke test, is the one used for the accept/reject call.
+
+\textbf{Tencent Hunyuan-MT-7B}, deployed as a Q4\_K\_M GGUF quantization via
+Ollama (the WMT25-winning model in its original bf16 release), scored 54.7 BLEU /
+74.7 chrF at 112\,chars/s using $\approx$6.6\,GB VRAM. This is rejected: 54.7 BLEU is
+\emph{below} even the smallest model in this study (NLLB-600M, \NLLBFLORESBLEU{}),
+a $>$12-BLEU gap from Hunyuan-MT-7B's published full-precision standing. On an
+8\,GB budget, 4-bit quantization loss dominates the larger model's raw capacity ---
+larger-but-quantized is not automatically better than smaller-but-full-precision.
+
+\textbf{MADLAD-400-3B} was re-attempted with a clean re-download to rule out a
+one-off corrupted download as the cause of its earlier exclusion. The re-downloaded
+checkpoint still fails a tied-embedding integrity guard added in this optimization
+pass (\texttt{shared.weight} $\neq$ \texttt{decoder.embed\_tokens.weight}), confirming
+the corruption is at the source rather than local to one download. The guard
+converts what was previously silent BLEU-0 garbage output into a load-time
+\texttt{RuntimeError}, so a future corrupted checkpoint fails loudly instead of
+producing an uninterpretable evaluation result.
+
+A final systems observation from this evaluation round: Ollama silently falls back
+to 100\% CPU inference (5.4\,tok/s) rather than GPU (58.5\,tok/s) if VRAM is already
+occupied by another process at model-load time, with no error or warning. GPU
+placement must be verified after load, not assumed from configuration alone ---
+a lesson that generalizes beyond this project to any Ollama-based deployment
+sharing a GPU with other workloads.
+
